WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.149 --> 00:00:05.349
How does a filmmaker show an idea?

00:00:07.360 --> 00:00:11.520
I believe in ancestry, you know and

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:14.780
and legacy. How things don't go away.

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:20.679
I want to show you a technique director Derek
Cianfrance uses to communicate abstract concepts

00:00:20.679 --> 00:00:26.130
visually in The Place Beyond the Pines, an
underrated film from 2013 that I’m about

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:27.470
to completely spoil.

00:00:27.470 --> 00:00:32.619
The Place Beyond the Pines is about the passing
of the torch, how our legacy effects our children

00:00:32.619 --> 00:00:34.270
and those around us.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:39.480
Our actions have consequences, and those consequences
echo out from us affecting those around us,

00:00:39.480 --> 00:00:41.160
sometimes for generations.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:45.780
And while this is something we understand
almost instinctually about real life, it can

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:48.690
be hard to convey that on screen.

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:54.550
You could edit a story non-linearly highlighting
the connections between actions and their

00:00:54.550 --> 00:01:00.830
consequences, but Cianfrance decided to show
Pines linearly, in three distinctive acts.

00:01:00.830 --> 00:01:05.750
So how do you show the relationship between
two events separated by minutes or hours of

00:01:05.750 --> 00:01:06.750
film?

00:01:06.750 --> 00:01:07.750
I think the themes in Pines are most strongly
communicated visually through Sean Bobbit’s

00:01:07.750 --> 00:01:08.750
beautiful cinematography, and the staging
and locations of each scene.

00:01:08.750 --> 00:01:13.100
How do you connect Luke Glanton, who’s only
alive in the first 50 minutes of the movie,

00:01:13.100 --> 00:01:20.740
and his son who’s only on screen in the
last 50.

00:01:20.740 --> 00:01:30.700
Like this.

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:35.670
In the director’s commentary Cianfrance
talks about how many of the scenes have echos

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:37.480
later in the film.

00:01:37.480 --> 00:01:41.920
Visual motifs and recurring locations show
how you can feel connected to someone’s

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:45.560
legacy even after they’ve been dead for
15 years.

00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:50.390
With shots like these we can connect a father
and son’s shared love of riding.

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:53.950
Their free spirits.

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:58.000
We can connect their crimes.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.120
Show how they develop similar friendships.

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:05.049
These visual connections helps the audience
understand the relationship between these

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:11.230
two figures who are hardly ever on screen
together.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:19.739
A scene towards the end of the film where
Jason visits the trailer is dad lived in 15

00:02:19.739 --> 00:02:25.579
years earlier, visually echoes the scene where
Luke sees the trailer for the first time.

00:02:25.579 --> 00:02:29.599
These two scenes, are an hour apart in the
film, but when they’re cut together you

00:02:29.599 --> 00:02:32.769
can see how clearly they interact with each
other.

00:02:32.769 --> 00:02:37.690
The feeling of connection that the characters
are feeling in that moment is palpable to

00:02:37.690 --> 00:02:38.969
the viewer.

00:02:38.969 --> 00:02:44.159
Our memory is triggered when we recognize
the look of the scene from before, and it

00:02:44.159 --> 00:02:51.049
puts us emotionally in the shoes of the characters.

00:02:51.049 --> 00:03:02.989
But Cianfrance doesn’t just use these echos
to connect father and son.

00:03:02.989 --> 00:03:07.049
He uses it to compare and contrast.

00:03:07.049 --> 00:03:12.269
Look at this scene where Jason eats ice cream
for the first time with his biological father,

00:03:12.269 --> 00:03:17.370
alongside a later scene where he icecream
at the same place, with the man that raised

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:18.590
him.

00:03:18.590 --> 00:03:23.629
The second scene could have happened anywhere,
but by placing it here, the spector of Jason’s

00:03:23.629 --> 00:03:31.970
biological father looms over the entire moment,
and we immediately know that this statement,

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:43.049
“I’m your real dad”, doesn’t find
it’s mark.

00:03:43.049 --> 00:03:48.760
The similarities between the scene where Luke
and Avery try to give money to Romina, help

00:03:48.760 --> 00:04:28.530
drive home how the conflict that entered her
life will Luke continue past his death.

00:04:28.530 --> 00:04:35.009
We can see, just through the visual subtext,
how this relationship has had an impact on

00:04:35.009 --> 00:04:37.169
this one.

00:04:37.169 --> 00:04:41.020
The scene where Bradley Cooper’s character
picks up Luke’s son from the crib after

00:04:41.020 --> 00:05:04.430
his death, echoes an earlier scene where Luke
comforted his held his own son.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:12.620
We can see how this child has had an impact
on these two men in similar ways.

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:18.669
Setting these two scene in a pool, reinforce
how where Avery had a strong connection with

00:05:18.669 --> 00:05:22.629
his dad, he lacks one with his son.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:32.550
Film is a powerful medium for storytelling
because it gives you some many options simultaneously

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.560
for how to communicate ideas.

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:40.610
Cianfrance uses these echos to almost get
inside your head at times, as the viewer we’re

00:05:40.610 --> 00:05:43.570
affected, but we might not even understand
why.

00:05:43.570 --> 00:05:47.250
I think the themes in pines are most strongly communicated visually

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:52.590
through Sean Bobbit's beautiful cinematography and the staging and locations of each scene.

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:58.000
I've only covered some of the echoes that are in the Place Beyond the Pines

00:05:58.300 --> 00:06:09.600
I’ll leave you with a side by side comparison
of the first and last shots of the film.

00:06:09.610 --> 00:06:18.389
The entire thrust of the story is summed up
in these shots.

00:06:18.389 --> 00:06:23.099
The generational evolution is made clear.

00:06:23.099 --> 00:06:32.669
Jason is similar to his father, it’s easy
to imagine him as a young Luke in this shot,

00:06:32.669 --> 00:06:41.549
but where Luke rides into a cage, Jason rides
off into freedom,

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:45.720
to the place beyond the pines.

